Date: Sun, 28 Feb 1993 21:38-EST
From: Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Steve Whitrow runs some simulations, but as far as I can tell he's still
fairly confused.  His problem is that he's trying to make simulations 
take the place of theoretical understanding.

First off, he verifies that the "60% half, 40% double" game is a win for
the bettor, unless he succumbs to a Martingale with no upper limit.
This is true and completely trivial.  Please let's not get into Martingales
here; they generate too much noise on sci.math and rec.gambling already.

Steve then concludes that he doesn't like the "log return" strategy, without
making any particular connection between it and his simulations.  In fact,
his simulations argue for the bettor to choose small bet sizes, just as
the log-growth strategy would tell him to do!  Tim May later calls a
Martingale a "log return betting scheme".   This couldn't be more wrong, and
I'm hard pressed to see how Tim made that connection at all!

I guess Tim was thinking that the log-growth metric would be overly willing
to accept a large low-probability loss.  But that's not correct; since
it's the log of your BANKROLL that is considered, the log-growth strategy
is extremely wary of losses which are a large fraction of your bankroll.

---

Let's go over what the log-growth strategy is about.  It's primarily a way of
choosing the optimum amount to wager on a positive-expectation bet, given a
limited bankroll.  The idea is to make a balance between expected gain and
the variance of the bet, by being less willing to lose your whole bankroll
and less interested in huge low-probability gains.  For each possible outcome
of the bet, you compute the logarithm of your new bankroll at that point and
multiply it by the probability of that outcome.  Adding these up gives you
the "expected log bankroll", from which you can subtract the log of your
current bankroll to get the "log growth of bankroll".   You then try to
maximize this quantity rather than your expected gain.

It should be noted that for any positive expectation bet, log growth of
bankroll is greater than zero if you bet a sufficiently small fraction
of your bankroll; and there will be one particular bet size, strictly
between 0 and the bankroll size, which has the maximum expected log growth.

In my last message on this topic, I computed the bet size which gives the
maximum log growth of the bankroll for the bettor in the "60% half, 40% 
double" game with bankroll B:  the answer was 0.2*B .

---

My own objections to the log-growth metric still stand; it's too conservative
in most practical circumstances.  But this recent criticism of the method 
is unfounded.


-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)

