From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

I think I see what Hal meant by the 'simulations being inadequate'. Say
the layer runs the computations on a machine that either crashes out to
zero after 10 ^ -38 or hits an overflow at 10 ^ 38. The bettor starts with
$1, and the arrangement is that if the balance goes below 10 ^ -38 he's lost
the $1. If it hits the 10 ^ 38 overflow he wins $10 ^ 38.

We know that the return is 10% of the total balance per round. But when
the log return is negative the random numbers are generated so that zero
is almost certain to be hit, and overflow _very_ rarely. I suspect that
the overflow would be hit on just enough occasions for the bettor to get
his 10% return. But it would only be in approximately 9.09 ee 37 of cases,
in other words the layer could be operating so that one series per second
had been completed for the age of the universe, and his chance of having to
make the devastating payout would still be about 1 ee 20 to 1 against. 

Unfortunately this method could not be applied to real life betting, as
you either need $10 ^ 38 of capital or credit from the bookie.

