Date: Mon, 1 Mar 93 12:12:08 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: BOMB: Will WTC Bombing Encourage Decentralization?

I spent the summer of '81 working in the financial district in NYC
(while waiting for a  real engineering job to start).  At that time
it was obvious why such a concentration of financial institutions
existed.  I worked in the check clearinghouse of MAnufacturer's
Hanover Trust, one of the big money-center banks.  I was working
in the Latin American department (totalling daily deposits by
country).  Banks from all over Latin America had checking accounts
at MHT, which functioned as their clearing-house to pay checks.

For example, when a cocaine grower in Colombia deposited his
million in cash with his local bank (the amounts of deposits
from tiny cities in these countries was otherwise unexplainable),
and then wrote a check to a Venezuelan aircraft dealer for a
new plane, the check went through the Venezuelan bank, MHT, and
the Colobian bank on it's way to clearing.  In the process the
MHT accounts for the two banks are adjusted to reflect the
transaction.

In an era when physical transport of checks was required for them
to clear, large buildings devoted solely to this function were
required.  There were similar buildings for the other money-center
banks, and for the Federal Reserve Bank of NY, which handled
domestic transactions among banks.

Similarly, when stocks are traded on the NY stock exchange,
Several days later Merril Lynch had to settle with the other
stock brokerages with physical stock certificates.  The certificates
were only a small fraction of the Exchange trading, since most
customer transactions by Merril Lynch customers could be handled
by re-registering shares ML already had in hand, but there was
some daily slop back and forth from mismatches of buys and sells
of a particular stock.  

In both the case of check clearing and stock certificate settlement,
there was a clear advantage to physical proximity, hence the
financial district.

Today, when many transactions are electronic, the justification
for proximity is less important.

Dani Eder

