Date: Mon, 1 Mar 93 17:46:22 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

   From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)

   Both log returns and linear returns seem flawed.  Is there any way out
   of this mess?  Can the two measures be combined to produce a better 
   combined measure?

Ultimately, there is no perfect measure, because your utility can be a
nearly arbitrary function of the amount of money you have.

The linear measure is "ideal" in certain senses, especially in that
the linear measure of a portfolio of many bets (or investments) can be
calculated straightforwardly from the linear measures of the component
bets.  (The proper way to deal with the bets under discussion here is
to wager $1 and leave it in play for 10 rounds.  Repeat 1,000 times.)

Dale

