From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: more ideas on anonymity

   From: ssandfort@attmail.com

   Can people be harmed by speech?  Maybe yes, maybe no.  But if so,
   that is the price that must be paid to avoid a far greater harm.
   Damnum absque injuria--there are some loses for which there is no
   remedy at law.

It seems to me that the interesting case is incorrect credit reports
-- the harm done to the subject can be quite large, but the subject is
not the buyer of the service (so there is no fraud involved).  Of
course, the merchant who buys the report is harmed also, but they may
be willing to put up with a higher error rate in reports in return for
a lower cost per-report.

(There may be alternative ways to organize the distribution of credit
reports which avoid this problem.  But this is the way it's done these
days, and it seems to show the common problems with distributing false
information about people.)

Dale

